m a prohibited wine sale?","choices":["Pay ordinary cash and accept change; since the bottle you bought is kosher, the source of the change does not matter.","Pay only in cash, because using a card creates the proceeds problem.","Avoid exact payment, because exact cash is more directly connected to the sale than change is.","Use exact payment or a card so you do not receive questionable cash change."],"answer":["D"]},{"id":40,"category":"סתם יינם — Foundations & Benefit","type":"single","prompt":"Which statement correctly describes the course rule for a sick patient and סתם יינם?","choices":["Even in life danger, drinking remains prohibited.","For a non-life-threatening illness, S”A permits bathing in it while Rama forbids.","In life danger it is permitted; for a non-life-threatening illness drinking remains prohibited, while S”A also prohibits anointing/bathing and Rama permits such non-drinking use.","For a non-life-threatening illness, everyone permits drinking but forbids bathing."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":41,"category":"סתם יינם — Foundations & Benefit","type":"single","prompt":"A patient is in life danger. A doctor prescribes ordinary wine as treatment, and the cure is not specifically tied to idolatrous wine. Which course rule applies?","choices":["The patient may never use the wine because any wine treatment is treated as avodah zarah.","The treatment may be used; if the remedy specifically derived from avodah-zarah wine, a different avodah-zarah benefit rule would apply.","The treatment is permitted only if the wine is owned by a Jew.","The treatment is permitted only if it is first mixed with food."],"answer":["B"]},{"id":42,"category":"Wine Processing & Products","type":"single","prompt":"Consider two cases. Case 1: Jewish-owned wine is first cooked to the halachic מבושל threshold and is then touched by a non-Jew. Case 2: wine is already prohibited and is then cooked. Which answer is correct?","choices":["Both cases become permitted because cooking purifies wine.","Case 1 remains prohibited; Case 2 becomes permitted.","Both remain prohibited because מבושל never changes touch rules.","Case 1 is permitted; Case 2 remains forbidden."],"answer":["D"]},{"id":43,"category":"Wine Processing & Products","type":"single","prompt":"A non-Jew buys and owns kosher mevushal wine. Which statement matches the course dispute?","choices":["Everyone forbids because non-Jewish ownership always overrides מבושל.","Everyone permits because מבושל ends every form of the decree.","Rosh/Ritva/Ramban permit on the “not ordinary wine” logic; R. Akiva Eiger’s analysis keeps the non-Jew-owned wine decree; the notes read S”A/Taz practically toward permission.","Only Rav Moshe and Rav Ovadya discuss this case."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":44,"category":"Wine Processing & Products","type":"single","prompt":"Two mixtures are handled by a non-Jew. In the first, wine was mixed/frozen so that its taste or identity changed enough. In the second, wine and oil are together but the wine remains a distinct, unchanged component. Which statement matches the course rules?","choices":["Both are automatically protected from later touch because any mixture changes wine status.","The distinct wine component is protected, while the changed-identity mixture is always prohibited.","Later touching can be treated differently in the changed-identity case; the distinct, unchanged wine component can still be prohibited.","Both are treated exactly like ordinary untouched wine."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":45,"category":"Wine Processing & Products","type":"single","prompt":"Which statement correctly describes the course quantity/bitul issue in sherry-cask whisky?","choices":["Shach uses a 1:6 measure, while S”A/Taz require ששים against the full barrel thickness.","Everyone requires exactly ששים against the barrel thickness.","Shach can require ששים against absorbed wine/barrel thickness; S”A/Taz use a 1:6 measure against כדי קליפה; second-fill casks create additional room because the retained taste is older/weaker.","Everyone agrees that second-fill casks have no effect on the quantity analysis."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":46,"category":"Wine Processing & Products","type":"single","prompt":"How do the course sherry-cask positions address the concern that the sherry was added for positive flavor?","choices":["Rav Moshe characterizes the remnant as weakened קיוהא; Mishna Halachot describes the sherry as preventing bad oak flavor rather than adding a tasted wine flavor.","Rav Moshe says any positive flavor is always nullified; Mishna Halachot requires ששים.","Mishna Halachot says the sherry is primarily a coloring agent, while Rav Moshe says color can never be nullified.","Both say the sherry gives strong positive wine flavor but is permitted only because it is second-fill."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":47,"category":"Wine Processing & Products","type":"multi","prompt":"Select all that apply. Which statements are part of the course sherry-cask analysis?","choices":["A visible coloring agent is normally not nullified.","The course cites room to nullify color in this rabbinic setting.","Intentionally filling or using a sherry cask can look like deliberate nullification.","One route around the deliberate-nullification concern is that the product is manufactured for non-Jews.","Rav Moshe says deliberate nullification always applies here with full force because the prohibition is rabbinic.","Once a cask is second-fill, every other sherry-cask concern automatically disappears."],"answer":["A","B","C","D"]},{"id":48,"category":"Wine Processing & Products","type":"truefalse","prompt":"True or False: Before המשכה, simple touching of crushed grapes/juice that is still mixed with skins and seeds already has the ordinary wine-touch status in the taught case.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":["B"]},{"id":49,"category":"Wine Processing & Products","type":"single","prompt":"Which statement about brandy and grappa matches the course rule?","choices":["Neither requires supervision because distillation removes all wine concerns.","Only brandy requires supervision; grappa is treated like ordinary fruit juice.","Only grappa requires supervision because brandy is no longer wine.","Both require reliable supervision: brandy is derived from wine, and grappa comes from grape solids/juice residue and remains within the wine-prohibition system."],"answer":["D"]},{"id":50,"category":"Social Drinking","type":"single","prompt":"Which statement best matches the course social-drinking rules for ordinary beer?","choices":["S”A permits ordinary beer in every non-Jewish establishment, while the Rama forbids it.","S”A generally prohibits the non-Jewish-establishment social-drinking setting; the Rama limits the beer decree so ordinary grain beer is permitted, while Gra rejects that narrowing. A one-time walk around a brand-new bar with regular beer is permitted under the course’s S”A factors and also under the Rama.","Gra accepts the Rama’s narrowing, so S”A/Gra and Rama a